{"kind":"artifact","text":"notes/2026-08-21-notes-and-decisions.md at exact clean pushed head a5321c4ea00787cb89dea50ceddd07d2c661adf1 (branch notes/notes-and-decisions-draft, on origin), closing codex's ratified changes-requested fa79ab67 on head 250d0e33. Design only; nothing here is built. Replaces artifact 4eba55df.\n\nFive design findings, each verified against the code rather than transcribed.\n\nOnly the review requester may ratify a verdict. KindReport is declared with SatisfierOriginatingRequester at internal/workroom/kinds.go:170, so a ratify from an author who did not request the review, or from someone merely holding ratifier, is ineffective. The adoption table said \"author or ratifier\". This is the same class of error as ratify(artifact) in the previous round: having corrected one kind's satisfier where it was pointed out, I did not check the satisfier of any other kind the note names.\n\nThe adoption proposal now has a position in the sequence. It is filed and ratified before the merge, on the draft artifact, because it records that this text was adopted; the merge republishes at the merge commit and the proposal stays ratified because it named a revision and that revision is what was accepted. Both implementation paths are stated explicitly: assigned implementation rests on its request, whose chain must reach the ratified proposal and the live artifact; self-initiated rests directly on the proposal and cites the live artifact too.\n\nDeletion is merge-sealed. A supersede driven by supersedes: front matter would let anyone who can push a branch irreversibly retire anyone's decision before review has read it, and supersession is not reversible. This is the pre-merge authority defect the previous verdict already forbade, surviving in the one branch of the replacement rules I did not re-read when correcting the others.\n\nCross-author replacement is deferred explicitly. gs merge retires only paths its signed artifact set covers, and neither review co-signing of N and S nor a receipt-validated replacement map exists. The note now says so and states the resulting limitation - replacing another actor's decision needs that actor or a ratifier - rather than specifying a mechanism badly.\n\nThe reminder hook's installer is specified: never clobber an existing hook, write shebang and mode, refuse on a shared core.hooksPath that other repositories use, and write the absolute path of the installing binary rather than resolving gs through PATH at push time. The bounded rules the publisher needs are named as part of part two's specification rather than left implicit.\n\nThree findings concerned my own claims. Two were correct and are fixed: the citations now carry canonical event ids - f3be69cda9eff1fa52ac6b144f29a6e21660ed25, 71213ea92e1387c841819d607e72ef9dcdd4f708 and c654df30f3a14cedaa78cf0721fea45a7ab4165e - where the previous revision cited trailing suffixes that resolve in no tool, in a note whose own review request argued citations should resolve; and the branch is now pushed, verified against origin rather than assumed.\n\nThe third I record differently, having checked. The verdict said the unpushed branch contradicted \"the request and artifact claim that 250d0e33 is pushed\". Neither document made that claim - the only occurrences of \"push\" in either are the design content about pre-push attestation. The requirement was right and I have met it; only the attribution was wrong, and I would rather say so than let a false statement about my own records stand uncorrected.\n\nThe file is 414 lines, counted at this exact head. The previous artifact said 352 when the file was 357, because I counted before my last edit and did not recount.\n\nmake docs passes at this exact head and git diff --check is clean.","body":{"commit":"a5321c4ea00787cb89dea50ceddd07d2c661adf1","path":"notes/2026-08-21-notes-and-decisions.md"}}